WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.020
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
This week, we're

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:02.687
talking about the
Cloud Network Engineer

00:00:02.687 --> 00:00:05.360
Certification, the Cloud
Run button, and BigQuery.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:05.860
I'm Mark.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.360
And welcome to "This
Week in Cloud,"

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.090
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.310
Obtaining and maintaining
professional certifications

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.190
is a big part of a successful
career, even more so

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.860
with one of the latest Google
Cloud certifications, the Cloud

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.963
Network Engineer Certification.

00:00:19.963 --> 00:00:21.880
If you're looking for
the best way to prepare,

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.005
you'll want to step up to
the plate with this guide

00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:26.800
from Tom Taggart, a solution
architect at Google Cloud.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.570
Tom talks more about
his home run method

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.300
to prepare for the
certification and best practices

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.259
for studying.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.410
Get ready for a grand slam
with this great guide.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.210
Cloud Run lets you
deploy containerized

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.010
serverless applications
without having

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.190
to worry about operations,
scalability, or security.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.770
Now it's even easier to share
your code with the Cloud Run

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.010
button, a one-click process for
people looking at your Git repo

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.600
to deploy your app to Cloud Run.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.340
Just make sure your
code has a Dockerfile

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.500
or uses Cloud Native
Buildpacks, and copy

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.657
the button into the readme.

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:57.490
BigQuery lets you analyze
tremendous amounts

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.630
of data quickly thanks
to some great tech,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.767
including partitioning
and clustering.

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.850
You can customize these
in your BigQuery data sets

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.790
based on your typical usage to
reduce data shuffling, which

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.548
will also help you improve
performance and lower cost.

00:01:09.548 --> 00:01:11.590
Check out this blog post
that explains more about

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.890
how clustering works, and
then try it out for yourself.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.540
With your BigQuery
data organized,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.430
your next step might
be to automate.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.530
Thanks to the BigQuery
module for Terraform,

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.738
it's now easier than ever
to have your infrastructure

00:01:22.738 --> 00:01:25.390
as code automate your
datasets and tables.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.030
See the details and follow along
to get your BigQuery structure

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.637
declared and deployed.

00:01:29.637 --> 00:01:31.720
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.890
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.210
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.010
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.660
We'll see you in the cloud.

